# Extra substance: a worked example per lesson (appended to the core material) and common misunderstandings.
E = {
"C001-L01": dict(ex=("A worked example: the anaesthetic",[
  "Consider what is said about a patient during surgery. Doctors monitor how deeply the patient is anaesthetised, because the aim is that the patient is not awake and does not feel pain. In everyday speech the patient is 'unconscious'. Now suppose a rare case in which a patient cannot move or respond but later reports having heard voices during the operation. Whether such cases happen, and how often, is a medical question. What the example shows is that three questions are in play: was the patient responsive (a behavioural question), was the patient aware of anything (a question about access to information), and was there something it was like for the patient (a question about experience)? A single word covers all three, and an answer to one does not automatically answer the others."]),
 mis=[("Consciousness just means being awake.","Wakefulness is one sense. People can have experiences while asleep, as in dreams, and can be awake while attending to nothing in particular. Researchers who ask the hard questions usually mean experience."),("Philosophers and scientists mean the same thing by the word.","They often do not. Philosophy tends to ask what experience is, neuroscience asks which processes accompany it, and AI research asks whether the question applies to machines.")]),
"C001-L02": dict(ex=("A worked example: pain and its description",[
  "Imagine that you and a friend both stub your toes. You each say 'that hurt'. A brain scanner shows similar activity in each of you. Does this show that the two experiences feel the same? The scanner shows what is common in the physical process. The words show that you each use the same label. Neither directly shows how it feels to each of you. This is not to suggest that the experiences differ, only that the evidence available to an outsider is of a different kind from the experience itself. It is why researchers who study consciousness are careful about the difference between what a person reports and what a person experiences, and why the problem is described as hard even for something as ordinary as a stubbed toe."]),
 mis=[("The hard problem says science cannot study consciousness.","The hard problem is a claim about one kind of question, why processing is accompanied by experience. It does not say that studying mechanisms, such as how attention or report works, is useless. Much of the field does exactly that."),("Everyone agrees there is a hard problem.","They do not. Some philosophers think the puzzle disappears once the functions are explained. The lesson presents it as a live position, not a settled one.")]),
"C001-L03": dict(ex=("A worked example: the blind spot",[
  "Each eye has a small region where the optic nerve leaves the retina and there are no light-sensitive cells. You do not see a hole in your visual field, because the brain fills in the gap using the surroundings. You can notice it with a simple demonstration: close one eye, fix your gaze on a mark, and slowly move a small object across the side of your view until it vanishes. The example shows that what you experience is not a direct copy of the input. It also illustrates why researchers separate the stimulus from the experience: the same missing input is present at all times, yet nothing is experienced as missing."]),
 mis=[("If a brain region responds to a stimulus, the person is conscious of it.","Studies reviewed in this lesson suggest that stimuli can be processed and can influence behaviour without being reported as seen."),("Perception is a passive recording of the world.","Perception relies on interpretation and expectation as well as signals from the senses. Illusions and filling-in show this.")]),
"C001-L04": dict(ex=("A worked example: the crowded room",[
  "At a busy gathering you can follow one conversation and ignore the others. If someone across the room says your name, you may notice it even though you were not attending to that voice. One reading is that attention filters, but some signals are strong enough to break through. Another is that you were aware of the other voices all along, faintly. A third is that part of the processing happened without awareness and what you noticed was the result. These readings predict different things, and experiments try to tell them apart. The lesson's point is that an ordinary experience can be described in more than one way, and that the choice affects what you think attention and awareness are."]),
 mis=[("What you attend to is what you are conscious of, so they are the same thing.","They usually go together. Whether they can be separated is debated, and the evidence on both sides is contested."),("Attention schema theory says attention does not exist.","The theory proposes that awareness is the brain's simplified model of attention. Attention itself, as a process of selecting information, is not denied.")]),
"C001-L05": dict(ex=("A worked example: reading a headline",[
  "Suppose a headline says 'Scientists find the seat of consciousness'. Ask what the study could have shown. It may have compared brain activity when people reported seeing a picture with activity when they did not. If a region differed, the careful description is that the region's activity is associated with reporting a picture. The next questions are whether the region is necessary (would removal or disruption abolish experience, and not just the report?), whether it is sufficient, and whether the result holds in other experiments and with other methods. A headline that moves from 'associated with' to 'is where consciousness lives' has added claims the study did not test."]),
 mis=[("A neural correlate is the cause of consciousness.","The term names an accompanying pattern. It leaves open whether the pattern produces, enables or follows experience."),("Brain scans can read whether someone is conscious.","They can show activity that tends to accompany reported experience. Interpreting a scan for an individual is a further step with its own limits.")]),
"C001-L06": dict(ex=("A worked example: one finding, six readings",[
  "Imagine a result in which a late burst of widespread activity follows a stimulus that people report seeing, and does not follow one they miss. A workspace theory can read this as the stimulus being broadcast. A recurrent processing theory might say the important event is earlier feedback activity, and that the late burst reflects reporting. A higher-order theory might look for activity that represents the person's own state. A predictive processing account might ask how the activity relates to prediction error. The same observation can be placed in several frameworks. This is why theories are compared on what each predicts in new experiments, not on whether each can be made to fit a result after the fact."]),
 mis=[("The Theory Chamber ranks theories from best to worst.","It does not. It lays out each theory's claims, sources and criticisms so that you can compare them."),("If a theory is listed in the Chamber, it has been confirmed.","Listing a theory means it is a serious subject of research. It does not mean the theory is correct.")]),
"C001-L07": dict(ex=("A worked example: the octopus and the thermostat",[
  "People disagree about whether an octopus has experiences, and few people think a thermostat does. What separates those cases? Some answers appeal to behaviour (the octopus learns, explores and responds flexibly), some to biology (a nervous system with many neurons), and some to theory (a view about what kinds of processing matter). Each answer depends on assumptions that are themselves unsettled. If a theory says what matters is the right kind of widespread information sharing, then the answer might depend on a system's architecture. If a theory says it depends on physical structure, a different answer follows. The example shows how a question about other beings is linked to the question about theory, and why the reasoning has to be explicit."]),
 mis=[("If a machine says it is conscious, we should believe it.","Fluent self-description can be produced by systems trained on human language whether or not there is experience. It is not strong evidence on its own."),("If we do not know whether machines are conscious, the answer must be no.","Not knowing is different from a negative answer. A careful position states what the evidence does and does not support.")]),
"C001-L08": dict(ex=("A worked example: applying the checklist",[
  "Take the sentence 'Brain imaging shows that dogs are conscious.' Which sense is meant? Probably experience. What was measured? Perhaps brain responses in dogs to familiar stimuli. Is that evidence about reports, activity or behaviour? Activity and behaviour, since dogs cannot report. What would the claim predict, and what would count against it? If experience goes with certain brain patterns in humans, similar patterns in dogs might suggest similar processes, though the link between those patterns and experience is itself under study. The source type matters too: a primary study, a review, or a news report. After working through this you may still think dogs are probably conscious, but you will be clear about which part of that belief rests on evidence and which on inference."]),
 mis=[("Having a method means the question is answered.","A method structures the thinking. It does not supply the evidence. The aim of the checklist is to see what a claim rests on."),("Admitting uncertainty is a weakness.","Stating what is unknown and what would settle it is part of responsible research.")]),
"C002-L01": dict(ex=("A worked example: three headlines",[
  "Consider three headlines: 'AI detects cancer', 'AI writes poetry' and 'AI beats world champion at Go'. In each, 'AI' stands for a different system, built differently, trained on different data, and evaluated differently. The first might be an image classifier trained on labelled scans, the second a language model, the third a system trained by playing games against itself. A result in one of these says little directly about the others. A reader who asks 'which system, what task, how was it tested?' will usually learn more from a story than one who treats 'AI' as one thing."]),
 mis=[("AI is a single technology.","It is a field with several methods. Rule-based systems, statistical learning and neural networks work differently."),("If a system is called intelligent, it can do many things.","Most systems are built for narrow tasks. A system that is excellent at one task may be unable to do a neighbouring one.")]),
"C002-L02": dict(ex=("A worked example: two ways to build a spam filter",[
  "A rule-based filter might say: if the message contains these words, mark it as spam. A person writes and maintains the rules, and spammers can adapt around them. A learning-based filter is shown many messages labelled spam and not spam, and adjusts itself so that it predicts the labels. It may pick up patterns no one wrote down, and it needs new data as spam changes. Both are useful, and the history of AI contains both approaches, sometimes combined. The example shows why the shift from rules to learning mattered: it moved effort from writing knowledge to collecting and checking data."]),
 mis=[("AI research went in a straight line to today's systems.","Different approaches succeeded at different tasks and periods. Several ideas were revived after being set aside."),("The 1955 proposal predicted today's systems.","The proposal set out a research programme. Whether and when its goals would be reached was uncertain then and remains a matter of debate for general intelligence.")]),
"C002-L03": dict(ex=("A worked example: learning to recognise handwriting",[
  "Suppose you want a program to read handwritten digits. In a machine learning approach you collect many images of digits with their correct labels, choose a model with adjustable parameters, and train it to reduce its errors. Then you test it on digits it has not seen, including handwriting styles different from those in the training set. If it does well on the training digits and poorly on new ones, it has not generalised. If it fails on a style that was never in the data, the problem is the data's coverage, not only the model. This shows why the data and the test matter as much as the algorithm."]),
 mis=[("A model that fits the training data perfectly is a good model.","It may have fit noise. Performance on held-back data shows whether it generalises."),("More flexible models are always better.","Flexibility helps up to a point. Beyond it the model fits noise and generalises worse, as the demonstration shows.")]),
"C002-L04": dict(ex=("A worked example: why the network needs hidden units",[
  "Think of four inputs: (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1). For XOR, the answers are 0, 1, 1, 0. A single unit makes one straight cut across this space, with the input points on either side. No single straight line separates (0,1) and (1,0) from (0,0) and (1,1). A network with hidden units can make several cuts and combine them. That is the small-scale version of a general idea: layers let a network build complex distinctions from simple ones. It is also why bigger and deeper networks can capture more patterns, with the caution that more capacity is not the same as better understanding."]),
 mis=[("Neural networks work like human brains.","They were loosely inspired by neurons, but they are mathematical models and differ from brains in many ways."),("A trained network contains rules a person can read.","Its behaviour is spread across many weights. Explaining a particular output can be hard.")]),
"C002-L05": dict(ex=("A worked example: why a fluent answer can be wrong",[
  "Suppose you ask a language model for a reference on a narrow topic. The model has learned that references tend to have authors, a title, a journal and a year, and it can produce text with that form. If it does not have a reliable record of a real source, the most probable-looking text can still be a source that does not exist. The sentence reads like a citation because the model has learned what citations look like. Whether the reference is real is a separate matter that the form does not settle. This is why you should check sources independently, especially for claims that matter."]),
 mis=[("A language model looks up facts in a database.","By default it generates text from patterns learned in training. Some systems add search or retrieval, which changes how they work."),("All AI systems work like chatbots.","Image classifiers, game-playing systems and language models are built differently and have different strengths and failure modes.")]),
"C002-L06": dict(ex=("A worked example: two systems, the same score",[
  "Suppose two systems each score 80% on a reading comprehension test. One does so by understanding the passages. The other has learned that the answer is usually in the first sentence, a pattern in this test's design. Their scores are identical. They would behave very differently on a test where the answer is elsewhere. The example shows why researchers build varied tests and look for failure cases, and why a single number can mislead. It also shows why 'understanding' is hard to define through tests alone: different internal routes can produce the same result."]),
 mis=[("A high benchmark score means a system has the human ability the benchmark is named after.","Names can overstate what is measured, and systems can exploit shortcuts or overlap with training data."),("The debate about understanding is only semantic.","The terms matter, but so does evidence about how systems behave on new problems and what they represent internally. Researchers disagree about both.")]),
"C002-L07": dict(ex=("A worked example: capability without a verdict",[
  "A chess program beats grandmasters. It is, by one definition, highly capable at chess. Does that tell us anything about whether it feels anything while playing? Most people think not. Now consider a conversational system that talks about its feelings. Its outputs are closer to the way a person would describe experience, and for that reason more tempting to take at face value. But a system trained on a great deal of human text may produce such language whatever is going on inside. To get evidence you would need something other than fluency: a theory that links particular properties to experience, and checks of whether the system has those properties. That is what the indicator-property work attempts, with the limits described in the lesson."]),
 mis=[("Intelligent systems must be conscious.","Capability and experience are different questions. The Archive treats whether intelligence can exist without consciousness as open."),("Searle proved that machines cannot think.","The Chinese Room argument is a contested argument about whether running a program suffices for understanding. It has attracted many replies.")]),
"C002-L08": dict(ex=("A worked example: a hiring tool",[
  "Imagine a tool that ranks job applicants, trained on a company's past hiring decisions. If past decisions favoured certain groups, a model trained to imitate them can reproduce that pattern even though no one told it to. Checking requires deciding what fair means (equal selection rates, equal accuracy, something else), measuring it on the relevant groups, and checking again over time. It also requires deciding who is responsible if the tool causes harm. Risk management frameworks encourage organisations to ask such questions before and after deployment. The example shows that many risks come from the data and the setting, not only the algorithm."]),
 mis=[("A machine cannot be biased.","Systems learn from data produced by people and can reproduce its gaps and biases."),("A risk management framework guarantees safety.","It structures the work of identifying and reducing risk. It does not make a system safe by itself.")]),
}
